{ "paragraphs": [
  { "start": 1910.4, "text": "My take on why designers are kind of frustrated and stressed right now is just there's less time to do design, the traditional design process. Things are just moving. Engineers are shipping five thousand PRs a day. To your point, this idea of building blocks and design system language and just a platform for people to build on feels incredibly important. Totally. It's very hard, but I think it's funny. We've had these conversations where I'm like, just do less. My encouragement has been do less. Don't design it if you don't have to. Maybe there's already an existing system or component or something that we can build on top of, or maybe we don't even need this feature. We actually just need to extend an existing feature." },
  { "start": 1960.9, "text": "I think that's an important thing. Part of that is thinking outside of the bounds of what you're working on, right, and thinking across everything. I think that is becoming much more important. The speed, for sure, is a reason why that's so important right now. Just to follow that thread, because that feels like a pretty big change in the design world. There used to be this traditional design process: prototype, mock, test, use research, iterate. Okay, cool. Ready for engineering, build it, iterate, ship it. And now there's just no time for that. The role, in her eyes, has just moved to big picture planning and just steering people in a good direction." },
  { "start": 2004.2, "text": "Does that resonate? How much has that changed in the world of a designer? I think that a hundred percent, we do work that way. It depends on what stage you're at in the process or how far along the product is. There are certain features for ChatGPT where we really obsess and spend a lot of time. We try a million things and throw out ninety-nine, and we finally ship one. The composer is changing every single day, and we try many things and throw out. But you could take that for many different things within ChatGPT." },
  { "start": 2052.9, "text": "Then there are other things like Codex, where, I don't know, a super app is a good example. We definitely are just trying stuff really quick. I think that we really embrace building in public, which is a different way of working. When you get comfortable with it, it's actually really gratifying or satisfying because you can try stuff and get instant feedback both internally and externally. But yeah, I mean, it is moving really fast. And I think it just depends. Certain things we really sweat the details and we do test. We A/B test, we'll do user research, and we kind of iterate and evolve a lot." },
  { "start": 2103.3, "text": "Then other things, we take big swings and learn quickly. I think the biggest thing is embracing that the technology might change in a month, and maybe certain interaction or setting or whatever that you needed doesn't need to exist, or now a new one does need to exist, or a new capability is there, or the latency profile has changed such that you can rethink this. All these capabilities are changing, or the intelligence has changed, and so you have to be really quick on your feet and maybe not be so precious about it until it's at the point where, okay, this is kind of hardened." },
  { "start": 2150.0, "text": "We don't think that this is necessarily going to change tomorrow because of the technology, so let's make this the best it can possibly be. We're thinking a lot about how we should evolve, really think about ChatGPT becoming deeply embedded in your personal life and how the app should feel. That's something that we're not going to necessarily design and ship overnight and just put it out there. We're spending time thinking about that, and we're trying a bunch of different ideas. We're putting it up on a whiteboard, doing some design in Figma, prototyping it. We're probably going to dog food a bunch of different ideas long before we ever ship something." },
  { "start": 2190.9, "text": "But that's a bigger project. Then there are other things where we're like, idea to ship in four hours. This all makes sense. Essentially, what I'm hearing is you pick your battles, and you focus the design effort on the things that will be durable and not change. Exactly. Yeah. It's hard to know what that is." },
  { "start": 2212.6, "text": "This episode is brought to you by Mercury, radically different banking loved by over three hundred thousand entrepreneurs and now with command. I've been a customer of Mercury's for over six years. I have never once thought about leaving. Mercury is basically what happens when banking is built by product people, not by bankers. They make it so easy, dare I say fun." }
]}

