{ "paragraphs": [
  { "start": 2875.5, "text": "There's a universal input that you can ask that knows when it needs to answer quickly or should be chatty, or whether it should go off and do work for you. That's a big excitement that we can get to: how can we continue to simplify the experience such that you shouldn't have to think about how this works? You shouldn't have to think about what model you're on or what sort of speed you're using. That goes back to balancing the different kinds of personas and user needs. Of course, we want to expose that for the right people, but we want to ensure that we can get a default experience that works great for most people." },
  { "start": 2914.0, "text": "I think it's going to be really proactive. I do think that we're going to get much richer input through voice and richer visual outputs that are more interactive. I think that you'll start to build more durable workflows with ChatGPT that you can use over and over. Right now, it often feels like you're starting from scratch each time, and we want to make this the tool that you can use to help you with whatever task you have. This all sounds wonderful. I can't wait." },
  { "start": 2948.2, "text": "Just zooming out a little bit, I want to talk a little bit to designers again. You're in this epic role right now. I imagine there are a lot of surprises as you entered OpenAI and took on this role. What's something you wish you'd known? What's something you wish you could whisper into Ian of day one to give you some advice? I mean, so many things. The first one is recognizing that we're really early. I was pretty intimidated coming to OpenAI, if I'm being honest." },
  { "start": 2970.4, "text": "I remember interviewing and thinking, oh no, I gotta, you know, GPT-4 had kind of recently come out, and I was like, what is all this stuff? I was using it, but I didn't feel like I had a true understanding of it. To be honest, I still have so much to learn. That idea of being early and figuring this out, I think I would want to hear." },
  { "start": 3014.1, "text": "The other thing I found, and for me, this is more personal. It's less about AI or anything, but I stepped into a really different role. Most of my career, I've managed before, but never at a scale that's growing so fast and at a company that is so different. Every day was something new. For a long time, I felt like I was kind of bad at this or I didn't know what I was doing." },
  { "start": 3052.3, "text": "Then I talked to friends who had led large design teams or were in a similar transition as me, and they all felt exactly the same. The problems I had were not unique. I guess this is more personal advice: at any stage of your career, there's that feeling of impostor syndrome and wondering what you're doing. Finding people you can talk to who are going through similar things is really helpful." },
  { "start": 3088.6, "text": "Finding that community has been helpful. Understanding that this is all so new and evolving quickly is extremely helpful to hear. Elena Furnea had this great post called 'Stop the AI Confidence Theater,' where she talks about how people keep posting all these things they've done with AI and how it changes their lives. It's just not true for most people." },
  { "start": 3105.6, "text": "Very few of these things are actually that great and working. It's better to be humble about it and say, 'Wow, I don't know. This is changing quickly.' Embracing it and seeing where it goes is important. That connects to what you said about a core component of people doing well right now: those who are curious and just poking around, trying these things again, because maybe it didn't work before, but now it does." }
]}

