{ "paragraphs": [
  { "start": 3783.0, "text": "But the production was incredible. The set design, everything about it, I thought was so unique. So if you have the chance to see it, I thought it was really, really good. I also just saw The Invite, which I thought was great. Have you seen it? Yes, also love it. It's so simple, but so good. That's what I loved about it; it's like a play, you know? It's just four people in one kind of beautiful apartment, and the set design's amazing. The writing, everything about it was incredible, and it's just kind of a wild ride. Knowing nothing about it, I thought it was incredible." },
  { "start": 3832.5, "text": "Excellent taste, sir. I love both those so much. And The Invite just came out; it's in theaters right now. Next question: favorite product you've recently discovered that you really love? I feel kind of like a typical Silicon Valley person recommending this one, but I finally switched to an EV after having many cars for a long time. I got a Rivian, and I respect the design a lot. They've really made it so that a lot of assumptions about how operating a car should work have gone away. You just walk up to it, and it unlocks. You don't have to turn on and off the car, and it's cool to see that evolving." },
  { "start": 3891.4, "text": "Of course, I love Waymo. If I could own a Waymo right now, that would be the most magical experience in the world. I'm waiting on bated breath for that to be broadly accessible for everybody. Do you have a favorite life motto that you find yourself coming back to in work or in life? Not necessarily, but I think trying to sum up how I feel about all of this is to have fun with it and just try stuff. I think don't feel like you have to fit into a specific box and know that it's perfectly alright to be very fluid about the way you work or think about things. Be flexible and adaptable." },
  { "start": 3935.5, "text": "It's not quite a life motto; it's more of a vibe of just trying to have fun with it and build great stuff. I should shift this question to what's just the vibe that's been helpful to you because I love that. It's very Sam Altman in terms of just building stuff. Exactly. Okay, last question. Something I love about your career history: if you look at your LinkedIn, you're a designer at Groupon. I love that you're at Groupon, and now you're heading design for essentially superintelligence. What's something you learned at Groupon that is maybe helpful these days building superintelligent AI?" },
  { "start": 3970.5, "text": "I love that. Groupon was a really fun company. Andrew Mason was an incredible CEO, and he brought such character to the product. It was kind of a brand-led product. We had a comedy team, a writer team, but they were all comedians, basically. The product really did not take itself seriously, at least in its early years. It didn't take itself seriously, and I think the brand really stood out. You weren't signing up for Groupon just because of the daily deal, although, of course, getting fifty percent off something was great. People loved that email because it was well written, funny, and had character." },
  { "start": 4038.9, "text": "I thought that early on, the company did a really good job with brand and embracing that side of things. The other thing, if I were to carry that forward, is that every company I've worked for has such unique DNA. The way you have to work changes a little bit based on that. Groupon was run one way, then I went to Instagram, which was very design and creativity focused. Kevin Systrom was an incredible designer, and he worked in that way with us, which was really cool and created a different way of working." },
  { "start": 4076.2, "text": "Then you have Sam Altman, who's a completely different way of operating. Learning that companies have these really different DNAs and understanding how to embrace them and leverage that has been something I've learned a lot from moving around and getting the opportunity to work at very different companies. It's also important to realize you're not going to change that. You're not going to come in and change that culture because that's very hard, especially if it's founder-oriented." },
  { "start": 4100.5, "text": "I think it's about finding, like, oh, wow. Like, at OpenAI,..." }
